    "['radius-server timeout [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 timeout 5', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 timeout 5', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `radius-server timeout 5` command:\\n\\n##\\nset access radius-server 192.168.7.249 timeout 5\\n##\\n\\nThis command sets the RADIUS server timeout to 5 seconds for the specified server (192.168.7.249) on a Juniper router. Note that Juniper requires the server IP to be specified in the timeout configuration, whereas Cisco applies it globally in this context.\", 'trans_template': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 timeout 5'}]]": 1,
    "['radius-server retransmit [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 retry 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 retry 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent for the Cisco `radius-server retransmit 3` command:\\n\\n##\\nset access radius-server 192.168.7.249 retry 3\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper combines the retransmit configuration with the radius-server definition\\n2. The command assumes the same RADIUS server IP (192.168.7.249) from the original configuration\\n3. Juniper uses \"retry\" instead of \"retransmit\" for the same function', 'trans_template': 'set access radius-server 192.168.7.249 retry 3'}]]": 1,
